### What is the Regular Expression Matching Problem?

Implement pattern matching where `.` matches any single character and `*` matches zero or more of the preceding element. The match must cover the entire input string, not just a substring.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh validates account reference strings against patterns. The pattern `a.b*c` matches references like "axc", "axbc", "axbbc", etc.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RegexMatching {
    public static boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length(), n = p.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];
        dp[0][0] = true;

        // First row: patterns like a*, a*b*, a*b*c* can match empty text
        for (int j = 2; j <= n; j++) {
            if (p.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                dp[0][j] = dp[0][j - 2];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                char pc = p.charAt(j - 1);
                if (pc == '*') {
                    // Zero occurrences: skip the x* pair
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2];
                    // One more occurrence: preceding char matches current text char
                    char prev = p.charAt(j - 2);
                    if (prev == '.' || prev == s.charAt(i - 1)) {
                        dp[i][j] = dp[i][j] || dp[i - 1][j];
                    }
                } else if (pc == '.' || pc == s.charAt(i - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isMatch("aab", "c*a*b"));    // true
        System.out.println(isMatch("mississippi", "mis*is*p*."));  // false
        System.out.println(isMatch("ab", ".*"));         // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
true
```

### Why Is the Star Case Difficult?

The star is the whole difficulty because it modifies the preceding element rather than standing alone. `a*` means zero or more `a`s, not zero or more of anything. This means the star looks back two positions for the zero case and consumes one text character for the one-more case.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class StarCaseExplained {
    // Pattern: "xa*b", Text: "xb"
    // Star takes zero 'a's: skip 'a*' -> match "xb" against "xb" -> true

    // Pattern: "xa*b", Text: "xaaab"
    // Star takes three 'a's: consume 'a' three times -> match "xaaab" against "xa*b" -> true

    // Two branches at star:
    // 1. dp[i][j-2]: zero occurrences (skip the x* pair)
    // 2. dp[i-1][j]: one more occurrence (preceding matches, consume one text char)
}
```

### How Does the First Row Work?

The first row represents matching an empty text. A pattern like `a*` can match empty text because the star takes zero occurrences. A pattern like `a*b*c*` can also match empty text if each star takes zero. We set `dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-2]` when the pattern char at j is a star.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FirstRowExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String p = "a*b*c";
        int n = p.length();
        boolean[] firstRow = new boolean[n + 1];
        firstRow[0] = true;
        for (int j = 2; j <= n; j++) {
            if (p.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                firstRow[j] = firstRow[j - 2];
            }
        }
        // firstRow: [T, F, T, F, T, F]
        //            0  a  *  b  *  c
        // dp[0][2]=T: a* matches empty (zero a's)
        // dp[0][4]=T: a*b* matches empty (zero a's, zero b's)
        // dp[0][5]=F: a*b*c requires at least one c
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
            System.out.println("dp[0][" + j + "] = " + firstRow[j]);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
dp[0][0] = true
dp[0][1] = false
dp[0][2] = true
dp[0][3] = false
dp[0][4] = true
dp[0][5] = false
```

### How Is This Different from Wildcard Matching?

| Aspect | Regex Matching (. and *) | Wildcard Matching (? and *) |
|---|---|---|
| Star meaning | Zero or more of PRECEDING element | Any sequence of characters (standalone) |
| Star dependency | Tied to previous char (x*) | Independent (matches anything) |
| Zero case | dp[i][j-2] (skip x* pair) | dp[i][j-1] (skip star) |
| One-more case | dp[i-1][j] if preceding matches | dp[i-1][j] (absorb one char) |
| Complexity | O(m*n) | O(m*n) |

```java
// Regex *: dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2] || (prevMatch && dp[i-1][j])
// Wildcard *: dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-1] || dp[i-1][j]
```

### What Are Common Mistakes?

The first mistake is forgetting the zero-occurrences case for star. The second is not checking that the preceding pattern character matches the current text character before allowing one-more.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: only handles one-more case, misses zero occurrences
if (pc == '*') {
    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j];  // BUG: no zero-occurrences check
}
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: both zero and one-more cases
if (pc == '*') {
    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2];  // zero occurrences
    if (prev == '.' || prev == s.charAt(i - 1)) {
        dp[i][j] = dp[i][j] || dp[i - 1][j];  // one more
    }
}
```

### What About the Recursive Approach?

The recursive approach with memoization is often easier to write and understand. We match characters from left to right, handling the star by branching into zero and one-more.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class RegexRecursive {
    private static Boolean[][] memo;

    public static boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        memo = new Boolean[s.length() + 1][p.length() + 1];
        return dp(s, p, 0, 0);
    }

    private static boolean dp(String s, String p, int i, int j) {
        if (j == p.length()) return i == s.length();
        if (memo[i][j] != null) return memo[i][j];

        boolean firstMatch = i < s.length()
            && (p.charAt(j) == '.' || p.charAt(j) == s.charAt(i));

        boolean result;
        if (j + 1 < p.length() && p.charAt(j + 1) == '*') {
            result = dp(s, p, i, j + 2)                    // zero occurrences
                  || (firstMatch && dp(s, p, i + 1, j));    // one more
        } else {
            result = firstMatch && dp(s, p, i + 1, j + 1);  // single match
        }
        memo[i][j] = result;
        return result;
    }
}
```

